-- title:
--   Coefficient embedding sends j(q) over ℚ to j(q) over L
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure. Write $\mathtt{jNum} \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ for the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$-type numerator built from `eisenstein4` and `dedekindEtaUnitInv`, and for a commutative ring $K$ let `jqModC K` be the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot \iota(\mathtt{jNum}\otimes K)$, that is, the product of the Hahn series $\mathrm{single}\,(-1)\,1$ with the image of $\mathtt{jNum}$ under coefficientwise reduction along $\mathbb{Z} \to K$, viewed as a Laurent series. Let `jq` be the same construction over $\mathbb{Q}$, namely $\mathrm{single}\,(-1)\,1$ times the Laurent series attached to $\mathtt{jNumQ} = \mathtt{jNum}$ mapped along $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$. Let `coeffEmb L` be the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to L((q))$ obtained by applying the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ to each coefficient. The theorem asserts that `coeffEmb L` carries `jq` to `jqModC L`: the two routes to the $q$-expansion of $j$ in $L((q))$, one factoring through $\mathbb{Q}$ and one reducing the integral series directly, coincide.
--
--   This is the compatibility of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ with base change of coefficients from $\mathbb{Q}$ to an arbitrary field of characteristic zero. It is used throughout the treatment of the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and of $q$-expansions of forms, for instance in computing degrees of field extensions generated by $j$ and in transferring $q$-expansion identities between coefficient fields.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_coeffEmb_jq.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_JqCoeff
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
